On Fri, 16 Oct 2026 20:01:36 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> What are the options for getting org-mode behavior with emails in
> notmuch? For example I'm looking at id:1792195756.4ee05afd82df@p7x.dev
> which I think would be easier to navigate if it were in org mode with
> headings and I could collapse them.

I found three options. I recommend the first and have posted it as
"[PATCH 0/2] Fold org-style headings in long mail" [1].

1. Fold "*" headings inside notmuch-show. A text/plain hook finds
   org-style heading lines with a regexp, turns each into a button
   with the outline face for its level, and hides its section with an
   overlay when you press RET on it. TAB already moves between
   notmuch's buttons, so it reaches the headings too. This is how
   pm-notmuch.el already highlights diffs. The mail stays in
   notmuch, and a fold never reaches past the end of its own message.

2. Turn on outline-minor-mode in the notmuch-show buffer. That gives
   outline's own TAB cycling, but it treats the whole thread buffer as
   one outline. The last section of one message would fold the next
   message's header away unless we wrote a custom
   outline-search-function that knows where messages begin. That is
   more code than option 1 for the same result.

3. Copy the body into a read-only org-mode buffer, or have agents
   send a text/x-org part that notmuch renders with org. This gives
   real org behavior, but it is the option your injection question is
   about (see below).

The message you cite would not fold under option 1, because it marks
its sections with titles underlined by dashes. Patch 2 of the series
asks agents to use "*" headings in long mail. A "*" heading also
reads fine in any other mail client.

> I wouldn't edit the document... it's really just about getting the basic
> expand / collapse behavior, and maybe some of the syntax highlighting.

Option 1 does only that. The highlighting is limited to heading
faces. Org's other markup, such as *bold*, =code= and src blocks, is
not highlighted. Each could be added the same way, with a regexp and
an overlay, if you want it.

> Although that makes me wonder - is it more likely to open up to an
> injection attack? That's something to be aware of. The incoming emails
> are untrusted and I don't want to run arbitrary elisp against them.

Yes for option 3, no for option 1.

Running org-mode on untrusted text has had real exploits. These come
from Emacs's own NEWS.29, which I checked on this system:

- 29.3: "Arbitrary Lisp code is no longer evaluated as part of turning
  on Org mode", and "Gnus now treats inline MIME contents as
  untrusted". The second covers org parts in mail.
- 29.4: "Arbitrary shell commands are no longer run when turning on
  Org mode."

29.3 also added an untrusted-content variable for code to check.
Those holes are fixed, but org-mode is large. From memory, not
checked here: it fontifies src blocks by turning on each language's
major mode and running that mode's hooks, and it has clickable
elisp: and shell: links. That is a lot of code to point at mail
anyone can send.

Option 1 never turns on org-mode or any other mode on the message. It
matches lines against a fixed regexp and adds overlays. Nothing from
the message is evaluated. The worst a hostile message can do is
make a line look like a heading, or put text under a heading. Every
section starts unfolded, so nothing is hidden until you fold it.

